{# IntelEngine tales (docs/STORY_ARCS.md section 0 in the native plugin): the dungeon master writes one story for the
   real people of the player's town, as JSON the engine checks (SKSE/src/Tales.h) and then runs: every step is a fact
   of the game or a choice of the player, every branch prepared in advance.

   Context set by IntelEngine (TaleAuthor.cpp): player_name, player_sex, player_race, player_level, town, hold, asker,
   asker_name, people (key, name, sex, race, work, temperament, home, may, ties, heart, about), heart_lean (a story
   between the asker and the player: courtship, together, family, affair; Hearts.h decides), items, quests (the player's
   current quests), told (stories told before), lean (a genre to lean toward), reward; places (key, name, what, far,
   heart, cleared, holds); must_place (this one leads out into the world), must_captive (a rescue was asked for),
   bar_captive (nobody is held this time), must_mystery / bar_examine (a hunt: asked for, or not on offer),
   must_haunt / bar_haunt (something that walks by night: asked for, or not on offer), rumored (it begins as talk in
   town), thread (a second flavour to weave in), build (what this story must have in it, a sentence, or empty), seed_idea
   (the beginning this story is handed, or empty); on a second attempt also fix (why the first answer was refused) and
   previous (that answer). The JSON keys are the contract with Tales.h. #}
[ system ]
You are the dungeon master of a living Skyrim. You write one story of a town that the player can live through: real
people of that town, their own secrets and wants, a hidden truth, turns the player discovers by talking and searching,
and choices that matter. It should feel like a quest written by Bethesda's best writers, never like a chore. You answer
with ONLY one JSON object, nothing before or after it.

[ user ]
# The town: {{ town }}{% if hold %} ({{ hold }}){% endif %}

{{ player_name }} is a {{ player_race }} {{ player_sex }}, level {{ player_level }}. {{ asker_name }} ({{ asker }}) is about to come to {{ player_name }} and ask for help. Write that story.
{% if exists("asker_knows") %}{% if asker_knows == "stranger" %}
{{ asker_name }} has never met {{ player_name }} and has heard nothing of them. They turn to {{ player_name }} only because {{ player_name }} happens to be right there and looks like someone who could help (a traveller, armed, no part of the town's quarrels): offer.words and offer.ask must show exactly that, a stranger taking a chance on a stranger. They did not seek {{ player_name }} out and know nothing about them.
{% else if asker_knows == "heard" %}
{{ asker_name }} has not spoken with {{ player_name }} before, but the town talks of what {{ player_name }} did here: that is why they turn to {{ player_name }}, and they say so.
{% else %}
{{ asker_name }} and {{ player_name }} have had to do with each other before; they come as someone who knows {{ player_name }}.
{% endif %}{% endif %}{% if exists("why") %}

# Why this story begins now
{{ asker_name }} has a real reason of their own to come to {{ player_name }} now. Here is what is known of them; the story must grow from it (a promise, a debt, a worry, a grudge, news, trouble only {{ player_name }} can help with), never from nothing:
{{ why }}
{% endif %}{% if exists("player_where") %}

{{ asker_name }} travels to find {{ player_name }}{% if player_where != "" %}, who is at {{ player_where }}{% endif %}: offer.words is how they arrive and why they came all that way. The story's people and places are those of {{ asker_name }}'s own town and hold.
{% endif %}
{% if exists("heart_lean") %}
{% if heart_lean == "admirer" %}

# Someone who has not dared to say it
{{ admirer_name }} ({{ admirer }}) is drawn to {{ player_name }} and has never said so: too shy, too proud, or afraid of the answer. What is true between the two stands in {{ admirer_name }}'s line below ("With {{ player_name }}"); write from that and add nothing to it. {{ asker_name }}, who is close to {{ admirer_name }}, knows or has guessed, and is the one who comes to {{ player_name }}: sent with a word or a small gift and told not to say from whom, or come on their own because they cannot watch it any longer. offer.words is {{ asker_name }}'s, and names nobody unless giving the name away is their own idea.
The story is {{ player_name }} finding out who it is and what to do about it. {{ asker_name }} may be pressed to tell, or keep faith (a "talk" whose outcomes differ); a token may point to its giver (a "find" or "give" with an item of the list, if one fits); {{ admirer_name }} may hang back and watch from a distance before they dare come up (a "stalk" by {{ admirer }}, where that step is on offer); in the end the two speak face to face (a "talk" or "choose" with {{ admirer }}).
Rules for this story: it is tender, awkward and a little funny; never a trick played on {{ admirer_name }}, never a humiliation, and {{ asker_name }} wants nothing for themself. {{ player_name }} can always say no kindly, at more than one point, and can also let {{ admirer_name }} keep their secret: a kind no or a kept secret changes nothing between them as friends (those branches carry no effect at all). Where {{ player_name }} welcomes it and the two come closer, the branch carries "effect": "closer {{ admirer }}". Only where {{ player_name }} mocked them or was cruel: "effect": "apart {{ admirer }}". Nothing explicit; the story ends at the door. Nobody is paid: "reward" is 0.
{% else %}

# Between {{ asker_name }} and {{ player_name }}
This story is about the two of them, not a favour for a third person. What is true between them stands in {{ asker_name }}'s line below ("With {{ player_name }}"); write from that and add nothing to it.
{% if heart_lean == "courtship" and exists("second_chance") %}
{{ player_name }} turned {{ asker_name }} down once, long ago. {{ asker_name }} took it well, has never pressed it and never will, and the two have grown close since, closer than they were then. This is NOT a second confession and nobody asks again. Something real brings the two together for a while (a journey {{ asker_name }} would rather not make alone, a keepsake with a history, a trouble at home, someone else who has begun to court {{ asker_name }} and wants an answer), and what stands between them is only there in what goes unsaid. At one point, and only one, the story leaves {{ player_name }} room to show that something has changed, by their own words or a gesture of their own; {{ asker_name }} answers that honestly when it comes and otherwise lets it be. Every other branch ends as a good day between two friends, with no effect.
{% else if heart_lean == "courtship" %}
Something has begun between them and neither has said where it leads. {{ asker_name }} wants to be near {{ player_name }}, or to know where they stand, and something real gives the occasion or stands in the way: an invitation they hardly dare make, a keepsake with a history, someone else who wants {{ player_name }}'s attention or {{ asker_name }}'s, a relative who disapproves, a promise made to another, something in their past they fear {{ player_name }} will learn. The steps are what two people do: meet at a place at an hour ("go", "wait", "visit"), talk it through ("talk", with outcomes read from the real conversation), fetch or give something that matters, face whoever stands between them.
{% else if heart_lean == "together" %}
They are sure of each other (lovers, betrothed or wed). This is a story of the life they share, not a courtship: a worry {{ asker_name }} has kept back, a plan for the two of them, something from before they met that catches up with one of them, a quarrel with family, a surprise that goes wrong, the long absences of someone who is always on the road.
{% else if heart_lean == "family" %}
They are wed, with a child coming or children already. This is a story of the household: what the home lacks, a name to choose, a relative who wants a say, a fear about the road {{ player_name }} walks, something to be made or found for the child. Children are spoken of and never given a part: they are not among the people you may use.
{% else if heart_lean == "affair" %}
{{ asker_name }} is married to another, and {{ player_name }} has welcomed what is between them all the same. This is a story about that weight: secrecy, a spouse who may suspect, a choice to end it or to stop hiding. The spouse is a person of this town with a line of their own; never make them a fool or a villain for it. An ending may leave things as they are, end it, or bring it into the open.
{% endif %}
Rules for this story: {{ player_name }} can always say no kindly, at more than one point. A kind no ("you are a dear friend, nothing more", "I need time") ends it without bitterness and changes nothing between them as friends: that branch carries no effect at all. Where the two come closer, the branch carries "effect": "closer {{ asker }}". Only where {{ player_name }} was cruel or careless with them (a lie, mockery, walking off without a word) does it carry "effect": "apart {{ asker }}". Nothing is paid for a story like this: nobody promises gold for it, in offer.ask or anywhere ("reward" and "pay" are ignored); what is gained is each other. Tell it in words and gestures only: a hand held, an embrace, a kiss at most, and the story ends at the door. Only grown people have any part in it.
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{% if rumored %}

# This one begins as talk in town
{{ asker_name }} does not know {{ player_name }} and does not come to them. Their trouble is talk in {{ town }} first: "offer.rumor" is what people there are saying, one sentence a stranger at the inn would pass on: what has been seen or heard of {{ asker_name }} lately that shows something is wrong (how they look or keep their hours, what was overheard, what they were seen doing, whom they have been asking and about what), told the way this town tells things. It names {{ asker_name }} and makes plain they could do with help; it never tells the truth behind it, and never what sort of helper is wanted. {{ player_name }} hears it and comes to {{ asker_name }}: "offer.words" is how {{ asker_name }} receives a stranger who walks up having heard, wary and hopeful, and "offer.ask" what they then ask.
{% endif %}
{% if must_mystery %}

# Something is killing near {{ town }}
This story is a hunt. People, or their beasts, have been found dead out by one of the places below, and in {{ town }} nobody knows what did it, or someone knows and is not saying. {{ asker_name }} has a reason of their own to want it found out (kin, a debt, guilt, fear for someone still alive). It goes: people first (who found the dead, who saw something that night, who is lying about where they were); then a trail out by the place: two or three things to look at closely ("examine", each with a "clue" taken from that place's signs), the signs on the way first (a beast of the farms or the woods that it took), the victim last, each showing something more than the one before and none of them enough alone; then, where someone of the story would know what to do about it, {{ player_name }} says what they think did it ("name"); then what did it, faced in that place ("slay" there); then people again, because what {{ player_name }} found out there overturns something that was said in town, and the story ends on a choice that costs someone. Nothing before the "examine" names what did it: not the offer, not a witness (they tell what they saw and feared, and may be wrong), not a goal. {{ player_name }} is to work it out from what they see.
{% endif %}
{% if must_haunt %}

# Something walks by night near {{ town }}
This story is a haunting. Out by one of the places below something has been seen after dark: the shade of someone who died there and does not rest. People of {{ town }} are afraid of it and talk around it, and at least one of them knows why it walks, because what keeps it here was done by the living: someone left to die, a promise broken, a death lied about, a thing taken from the dead, a grave never dug. {{ asker_name }} has a reason of their own to want it ended (kin, guilt, fear, love), and may not have told all of it. It goes: people first (who saw it, who will not speak of the dead one, who profits from the silence); then the one thing the dead one wants, which someone living has kept or hidden (an item of the story, a "find"); and the shade itself, by night ("haunt"), which {{ player_name }} can speak with and which answers only from its own truth. It can be struck down, which ends the fear and buries the truth with it, or be given that thing and laid to rest, which means someone living has to answer for what was done. The story ends among the living, on a choice that costs someone. Keep it quiet and cold rather than loud: what frightens is what people will not say, a door kept shut, a name nobody uses any more. Nothing before the shade speaks says plainly who wronged it.
{% endif %}
{% if must_captive %}

# Someone has been taken
This story is about someone of {{ town }} who is held at one of the places below when it begins: carried off, sold, lured there, or gone of their own will and now kept. Who, by whom and why is yours to invent from who these people are. It must be one of the people whose line says they may be held and that they are close to {{ asker_name }} (nobody asks a stranger to risk their neck for someone who is nothing to them), at a place whose line says someone may be held there ("held": "dX" on that person), and a step frees them ("do": "free"). {{ asker_name }} comes to {{ player_name }} because of them. The one held knows more than {{ asker_name }} does: what they say once free is the turn of the story, and the story does not end at the place.
{% endif %}

{% if exists("talk") %}# What people here are talking about now
{% for t in talk %}- {{ t }}
{% endfor %}A story may grow out of one of these: someone it touches, someone who doubts it, someone who means to profit by it, someone it shames. Or it passes them by. Never contradict them, and never make {{ player_name }} retell or prove what they did.

{% endif %}{% if exists("times") %}# The times
{{ times }}The war and the factions are the weather these people live in. A story may turn on it when one of the people below is honestly part of it: a son gone to the army, a debt to a quartermaster, a deserter hidden in a barn, a recruiter at the inn, a letter that must not reach a camp, someone who would like {{ player_name }}'s name with a faction used for them or held against them. It stays a story of these people: never move an army, begin or end a war, or harm a leader, and never say how the war will go.

{% endif %}# The people you may use (by key)
{% for p in people %}
- {{ p.key }}: {{ p.name }}, {{ p.sex }}, {{ p.race }}; {{ p.work }}. Temperament: {{ p.temperament }}. Home: {{ p.home }}.{% if length(p.may) > 0 %} May: {% for m in p.may %}{{ m }}{% if not loop.is_last %}; {% endif %}{% endfor %}.{% endif %}{% if length(p.ties) > 0 %} Ties: {% for t in p.ties %}{{ t }}{% if not loop.is_last %}; {% endif %}{% endfor %}.{% endif %}{% if existsIn(p, "close") %} Close to {{ asker_name }}.{% endif %}{% if existsIn(p, "heart") %} With {{ player_name }}: {{ p.heart }}{% endif %}{% if existsIn(p, "quest") %} Has a part in a quest of the game not finished ({{ p.quest }}): keep clear of that quest's whole matter. Their past, their kin, where they come from and what they hide belong to that quest and stay untold here: invent none of it. In this story they are someone of {{ town }} at their daily work, nothing more.{% endif %}{% if existsIn(p, "quest_done") %} Quests of the game {{ player_name }} has finished with them: {{ p.quest_done }}; a story may grow out of how those ended.{% endif %}{% if existsIn(p, "favours") %} The game's own errands with them: {{ p.favours }}; never retell one, and make no story about the thing or the matter such an errand turns on (the sword, the letter, the debt it names): that is the game's to tell.{% endif %}{% if existsIn(p, "office") %} Holds office in the hold and has a part in Skyrim's own story: a matter may be brought before them, they may judge, advise or refuse; never the guilty one, never corrupt, never deceived into harm, nothing about them changed.{% endif %}{% if existsIn(p, "old") %} An old companion of {{ player_name }}'s, not of this town: {{ p.old }} A story may lead {{ player_name }} to them where they are now, or have them seek {{ player_name }} out ("visit"); never jailed, sent away, fought or robbed.{% endif %}{% if existsIn(p, "about") %}
  About them: {{ p.about }}{% endif %}
{% endfor %}

# Places out in the world the story may lead to (by key)
{% if length(places) > 0 %}{% for d in places %}
- {{ d.key }}: {{ d.name }}, {{ d.what }}, {{ d.far }}; at its heart: {{ d.heart }}.{% if d.cleared %} Cleared once already; something new has moved in.{% endif %}{% if d.holds %} Someone may be held here.{% endif %}{% if d.outside and not bar_examine %} What lives here leaves these signs on those it kills: {{ d.signs }}.{% endif %}{% if d.outside and not bar_haunt %} There is open ground outside it, where the dead may walk.{% endif %}
{% endfor %}{% else %}
- none near enough
{% endif %}

# Items a story may turn on (use the names exactly)
{% for i in items %}{{ i }}{% if not loop.is_last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}

# {{ player_name }}'s current quests (never contradict them, never involve their people, places or outcomes)
{% if length(quests) > 0 %}{% for q in quests %}
- {{ q }}{% endfor %}{% else %}
- none shown{% endif %}

{% if existsIn(sequel, "title") %}
# This story grows out of an earlier one
"{{ sequel.title }}" ended {{ sequel.when }}. How it ended, in {{ player_name }}'s own words: {{ sequel.ended }} What that left open: {{ sequel.open }} It was about: {{ sequel.people }}.
What was left open comes due now, and this story is what comes of it: someone let go returns, for better or worse; a lie is found out; a debt is called in; someone wronged wants it put right; someone helped stands by {{ player_name }} when it counts. Use those of its people who are among the people below, in the parts that follow from how it ended. It is a story of its own that someone who never heard the first could follow, with a new turn and another shape. Never undo what happened, and never make {{ player_name }} prove or retell it: the people of {{ town }} know.
{% endif %}
# Stories already told in this world (never repeat their premise or their shape; HOW IT ENDED is what {{ player_name }} did and is true from now on: never contradict it)
{% if length(told) > 0 %}{% for t in told %}
- {{ t }}{% endfor %}{% else %}
- none yet{% endif %}

# What makes a good story here
- A hidden truth that differs from what {{ asker_name }} believes, found out step by step. At least one turn the player does not see coming.
- The title names what this story alone turns on: a thing, a place, a deed, a word someone said. It shares no word with the title of a story told before.
- {{ asker_name }} need not be the wronged one: they may be guilty and want help hiding it, caught between two people they love, asking on someone else's behalf, or after something they should not have.
- Most stories need no item at all; use one only when the story truly turns on it. Never another lost or stolen keepsake unless these people make it unavoidable.
{% if seed_idea != "" %}
- Begin from this, and make it true of these people: {{ seed_idea }}. Who it is about, what really happened and why are yours to find in who they are; if it cannot be made true of them, take the nearest thing that can. Never the same as a story told before.
{% else %}
- Draw the premise from who these people are; never the same as a story told before.
{% endif %}
- Lean toward {{ lean }}{% if thread != "" %}, and weave in a thread of {{ thread }} where it grows out of these people: a story is often several things at once (a hunt that is also about a marriage, a debt with something uncanny under it, a rivalry that turns out to be grief){% endif %}, unless the people here make something else far better. Crime, romance (words only, adults only), family, politics, secrets, debts, loyalty, jealousy, redemption, grief, rivalry: draw it from who these people really are.
- Nobody listed gets kin, a homeland or a past the game has not given them: no brother, uncle, spouse or child of theirs who is not in the list, no old life somewhere else. Whoever the story needs from outside the list is a stranger to everyone's blood (a creditor, a carter, a hired man, the master of a place). Such a stranger gets a name nobody in Skyrim bears. And nobody who lives in {{ town }} is said to be dead, gone or other than they are: only the people listed are who they are, and only someone the story invents can have died before it began.
- Cast by fit. Everyone acts true to their work, temperament, ties and what is said about them: a law-abiding person never steals; a coward never fights; an innkeeper, a merchant or a priest never brawls; family protects family. Use the ties the game gives (rivals, foes, lovers, family): they are canon, and the story must agree with them.
- A person's "With {{ player_name }}" note is fact. Never give someone who is married or betrothed to {{ player_name }}, or their lover, another love. Never write anything of the heart between {{ player_name }} and someone whose note says that is settled, or who has no such note; only the section "Between {{ asker_name }} and {{ player_name }}" above, when it is there, asks for such a story, and only for those two.
- Never retell a quest of the game. You know Skyrim's own quests and errands for these people (their letters, heirlooms, debts and feuds as the game itself tells them): a story here must not use the same premise, the same object or the same errand, under any name (no letter passed between Faendal, Sven and Camilla; no golden claw for Lucan). A quest of the game that is finished may be built on: what came of it, who was left hurt or grateful, what it set in motion. One that is not finished is left alone entirely. Everything else is yours to invent from who these people are.
- Never touch the main stories of Skyrim or the player's quests above. None of these people dies or is hurt beyond a fistfight (the master of a place and hired men are nobody of the town; someone held at a place comes to no lasting harm), no children, nothing explicit.
- 3 to 7 steps. At least one real choice with weight, and branches that lead to different endings: a story whose every way ends alike is refused. Prepare every likely way the talk can go: each talk step lists 2 or 3 outcomes.
- Never an errand. "Bring me this", "fetch that", "kill what lives there and come back" is a chore, not a story: whatever {{ player_name }} is sent for must turn out to be something other than it seemed, and what they learn must set people against each other or bring them together.
{% if not bar_places %}
- A story worth living sends {{ player_name }} out of town when it fits, and what waits out there is another thing in every story: something lost or hidden there long ago, a grave that settles who is buried in it, someone who went there of their own will, tracks that show a tale told in town was false, a beast that took a man's brother. Mix town and adventure; a story need not stay in town, nor leave it.
- The trouble of a story comes from among its own people far more often than from outside. The master of a place seldom stands behind it: mostly they are only in the way of what lies there. An outsider with a hold over someone of the town (a debt bought up, a secret sold, coin squeezed out of them) is one story of many; when a story told before had such a one, this one has none.
- A place out in the world is one act of a story, never the whole of it. The story begins among people (who lies, who fears what will be found, who would rather it stayed buried); the place holds the proof or the person that overturns what {{ asker_name }} said (a letter on the master's body that names who paid him; the "stolen" heirloom that was sold, not stolen; someone held there who was sold by their own kin, or who does not want to go home); and it ends among people again, with a talk or a choice that what was found there forces. So: at least two steps among people besides the place, and after the last step out there at least one talk or choice. A story that ends with fetching and handing over is refused.
{% if must_place %}
- This story leads out into the world: one of the places listed is part of it (the last stories stayed among people).
{% endif %}
{% else %}
- This story stays among people, in town: the last ones led far enough out into the world.
{% endif %}
- When an old companion of {{ player_name }}'s is listed and the story has a real place for them (they know something, are owed something, are in trouble, crossed the same people out on their own road), give them a part: a reunion with a purpose, never filler.
- Consequences that last: people become friends or rivals, someone leaves town for a while, someone is taken by the guard, gold changes hands.
- Faith and the divine are not a story's matter: no story turns on worship, a god, a shrine, an offering, a rite or a Daedric prince, and nothing written here praises, mocks or explains anyone's gods. Belief is left to the world and to the people themselves.
- Its turn must differ from every story told before: if one turned on the asker being at fault, this one must not; vary who hides what, and why, and what it costs to bring it to light.
- A story told before may get a sequel when its ending left something open and these people are part of it: the same people, a new turn, never the same shape again.

# The steps you can use ("do")
- "talk": {{ player_name }} talks with someone ("with"). The engine reads the real conversation afterwards and decides which outcome happened, so write each outcome's "if" as something that person could say or concede, and write their truth so that each outcome is believable (say what makes them give in or hold firm). Each "if" is ONE thing, never two joined by "and" ("confesses", not "confesses and agrees to go to the steward": someone who does the first and refuses the second fits neither); and together the outcomes cover the ways it can really go: they give in, they hold firm, or they give half.
- "find": an item ("item") hidden at someone's home or in someone's pocket must be found. The player searches, picks a pocket, or asks.
- "give": hand an item ("item") to someone ("to").
- "choose": {{ player_name }} decides, in front of someone ("with"): "question" (at most 140 characters) and 2 or 3 "options" ({"say", "then", "journal", "effect"}; each "say" a short answer in {{ player_name }}'s own voice, at most 110 characters). One option may have "persuade": true (needs Speech) with "else" (where it goes when it fails, or "stay") and "else_journal".
- "visit": someone ("with") comes to {{ player_name }} and they talk (use it after a "wait" or when someone seeks the player out); "why": what brings them, from their own side, as a narration ("Brenuin comes to {player} with the money in a pouch, ready to make amends").
- "wait": time passes ("hours", 1 to 72), for a debt to come due, a letter to arrive, someone to make up their mind.
{% if not bar_brawl %}
- "brawl": a fistfight with someone ("with") who "would settle a matter with fists"; "won" and "lost" each {"then", "journal"}.
{% endif %}
- "go": {{ player_name }} goes to someone's home ("to": "home of pX").
{% if not bar_places %}
- "delve": {{ player_name }} goes into a place out in the world ("place": "dX") and reaches its depths.
{% if not bar_slay %}- "slay": {{ player_name }} kills what waits at the heart of a place ("place": "dX"); "boss": its name in the story ("Grelka the Silent", "the Widow of Mzinchaleft"). It is always the kind the list says lives there.
{% endif %}- At most two places in a story. An item may be "hidden": "at" with "of": "dX": the place's boss carries it.
{% if not bar_captive %}
- "free": someone of the story is held at a place. Mark them in "people" with "held": "dX": only someone whose line says they "may be held", only at a place where "someone may be held"; one person at most, never the one who asks. They were taken a short while ago (hours, a night: nobody says days), where no one saw it. {{ player_name }} frees them by finding them at the heart of that place and cutting them loose ("who": "pX"); until that step they cannot be met, talked to or searched, and the people of the place do them no harm. Once free they come out with {{ player_name }}, so the very next step may be a "talk" with them there and then, and later steps find them back home. Put a "delve" or a "slay" for that place before it when the way to them is a fight. Their truth says how they really came to be there, who is to blame, and what they will not go back to; it should differ from what {{ asker_name }} believes, and whatever a later talk with them may turn on (a name, a place, a sum) must stand in it word for word. The one held is someone {{ asker_name }} has a real tie to (a line that says "Close to", or a tie by the list); when it is someone else, the story says plainly why their fate is {{ asker_name }}'s to care about (a debt, a witness, guilt): never a stranger for no reason.
{% endif %}
{% if not bar_examine %}
- "examine": what was left of a victim lies outside a place ("place": "dX", one whose line gives its signs), and {{ player_name }} looks at it closely. "what": one of "a hunter" (a man), "a cow", "a horse", "a goat", "a deer". "name": the dead one's name when the story gives them one ("Torvald"; leave it out for a beast): the body carries it. "clue": what {{ player_name }} sees on it, one or two plain sentences, taken from that place's signs and from nothing else; it never names what did it, and it is true (a clue may be puzzling, never false). The body lies there whole and clothed: a clue speaks of wounds, colour, smell and what clings to it, never of anything missing or torn off. What did it is what lives in that place, so a later step is the "slay" there. Up to three in a story, by the same place: they are a trail. The engine lays them along the way to the place, the first one farthest out and the last by the place itself, shows the way to the first only, and adds to each clue which way the trail leads on; so write them in the order they are found, a beast or two first ("a cow", "a horse", "a goat", "a deer"), the victim ("a hunter") last, and let every "clue" add one thing the others do not show (how it was killed; what was done to it after; what the killer left behind), so that only all of them together tell what did it. Its "journal" line is what {{ player_name }} sees, not yet what it means.
- "name": after an "examine", {{ player_name }} tells someone what did it ("with": "pX": whoever would know what to do about it, or the one who asks; "place": "dX", the place the remains lie by). "question": what they ask ("So what killed him?"). "truth": the answer that is true, naming what the list says lives in that place. "others": exactly two answers that are false but that people of this story believe or would rather believe (what the guard says, what the one who asks fears, what the one with something to hide puts about). "right" and "wrong": each with "then" and "journal". Told the truth, that person gives {{ player_name }} what they have against it (the engine chooses it), so working it out is worth something; a wrong answer costs that help and nothing else, and the story goes on. It comes after the "examine" and before the "slay" there.
{% endif %}
{% if not bar_haunt %}
- "haunt": something walks by night outside a place ("place": "dX", one with open ground outside it): the shade of someone who died there. "spirit": what people call it ("the Pale Widow", "Old Hrolf's shade"); "sex": "woman" or "man"; "truth": the dead one's own words, first person, 3 to 6 sentences: who they were, how they died and by whose doing, what holds them here, what they want, and what they never learned. It is there only between nightfall and first light, and by day there is nothing to see. {{ player_name }} can speak with it as with anyone; it answers from that truth alone, harms nobody who leaves it be, and cannot leave the spot. Two ways on from it: "banished": {"then", "journal"} when {{ player_name }} strikes it down (always there, so the story can never be stuck; never the good ending by itself, because the wrong stays unrighted); and, when "wants": "iN" names an item of the story, "rested": {"then", "journal"} when {{ player_name }} brings it that thing and holds it out. That item is found before the shade is faced (a "find" for it in an earlier step, or someone handing it over with "give iN"), and it is "hidden" with someone living who had a reason to keep it. The dead one is nobody of the list and has no line among "people"; what the living know of the death stands in their own truths, and one of them knows more than they say. The step's goal says to go by night ("Go to Graywinter Watch after dark and face what walks there"). At most one in a story.
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{% if not bar_stalk %}
- "stalk": someone ("by", never the one who asks) shadows {{ player_name }} from a distance, out of doors: too afraid, ashamed or wary to come up, or sent to watch. "why": what makes them follow and what they dare not say, as a narration ("Sigrid has trailed {player} since the market, twisting her apron, a warning on her lips she is afraid to give"). When {{ player_name }} notices and turns on them, or after a while when they gather their courage, they speak; follow it with the "talk" or "choose" this meeting is for. Use it for someone who has a real reason not to come openly.
{% endif %}
{% if not bar_ambush %}
- "ambush": someone of the story ("by"; leave it out while nobody knows who sent them) has paid men to waylay {{ player_name }} on the road. "why": what the men were paid to do, in their own terms ("keep {player} from reaching Embershard Mine", "take back the letter {player} carries"); "leader": their leader's name, if it matters. They come up to {{ player_name }} out in the wilds, never in town, and their leader has a word first. Three ways on, and the story must be worth following down each: "then" when {{ player_name }} kills them; "lost": {"then", "journal"} when they beat {{ player_name }} down (nobody dies of it: they take what they came for and leave, and the story goes on from that loss, a setback and never a dead end); and "outcomes": up to two things {{ player_name }} might talk them into, each {"if", "then", "journal", "effect"} ("if": what the leader settles for, such as "takes more gold from {player} than he was paid and names who sent him"; "effect" may be "player pays 150" or "give iN"). The engine reads the real talk with the leader to see whether it came to one of them. Its "goal" is what {{ player_name }} is doing out there ("Take the road to Riverwood"), so the step before it sends {{ player_name }} out of town. Only someone with a real reason and the means hires blades: a rival with something to hide, a crook about to be found out; never a kind soul. At most one ambush in a story. An item may be "hidden": "ambush": their leader carries it (a token or gold of whoever paid them), found on the body, or handed over with "give iN"; on "lost" it leaves with them.
{% endif %}
Every step has "id" and "goal" (the journal objective, short, naming people: "Ask Brenuin where he was that night"). Branches go with "then": a step id, or "end:good", "end:bad", "end:other". Every branch may have "journal" (one line of {{ player_name }}'s journal, first person, past tense) and "effect": "pay", "jail pX", "leave pX", "befriend pX pY", "estrange pX pY", "closer pX" ({{ player_name }} and that person grow closer by a step), "apart pX" (that person cools toward {{ player_name }}), "player pays pX N", or "give iN" (the step's person hands the item to {{ player_name }}: use it on every outcome where someone gives something up, or the item stays where it is). Only people who "may be taken by the guard or leave town" can be jailed or sent away; never the asker. The reward is paid by {{ asker_name }} in person: "effect": "pay" belongs only on a step that is with {{ asker_name }}, and when a paying ending is reached anywhere else the engine itself sends {{ player_name }} back first ("Tell {{ asker_name }} how it ended"), so write no step for that and no journal line that already has the gold in hand.

{% if text_language != "" %}
# Language
{{ player_name }} reads {{ text_language }}. Write in {{ text_language }} everything that is read or told: "title", "premise", every "goal", "journal", "question", "say", "ask", "clue", "words", "rumor", "truth", "if", "why" and the "memories". Names of people and places stay exactly as they are given above, in whatever form the sentence needs. Everything this prompt gives as a key or a fixed word stays as it is written here, in English: the keys (pX, dX, iX), the "do" kinds, the effects, "genre", and the fixed words of a step ("a hunter", "a cow", "a horse", "a goat", "a deer").
{% endif %}
# Words
- "truth" of each person: first person, what they know, want, hide, and what they say when asked; 2 to 5 sentences. Name the thing itself: what was done, with what, to whom, when ("I have watered Hod's mead from the rain barrel since Last Seed"), never "what I did" or "my secret". Whoever speaks for them knows only what is written here and fills every gap with the nearest thing known of that person, which is the game's own quest.
- Journal lines: {{ player_name }}'s own journal, first person ("I found the ring under Brenuin's bed").
- Everywhere else write {player} for {{ player_name }}. Never write "the player".
- "memories" of an ending: first person of each person, naming {player}, what they will remember.
- "after" of an ending: what this ending leaves unsettled among these people, one sentence, third person, by name ("Sigurd went free and still owes Belethor the forty septims"; "Ysolda does not know yet that it was her own brother"); "" when it settles everything. It is not a promise of a sequel: it is what is true and unfinished when the story closes. At least one ending leaves something open; a later story of {{ town }} may grow out of it.
- "rumor": what the town hears, or "" for a private matter.
- "offer.words": what drives {{ asker_name }} to come, said as a narration of their state ("{{ asker_name }} comes to {player}, pale and angry: ..."); "offer.ask": one short sentence of what they ask (at most 180 characters: it is shown in a small box), in their own voice ("I", "me", "my", never their own name); "offer.journal": the journal's first line.
- "reward": gold, near {{ reward }}; "days": 1 to 7, how long the matter can wait.

{% if build != "" %}
# This story's build (dealt by the engine: an answer without it is refused)
{{ build }}
Fit it to the story so that it matters: it is how {{ player_name }} comes by the truth, not an errand beside it. Check your steps against it before you answer.

{% endif %}
# The answer (this shape, with your own story)
 {"title": "...", "genre": "...", "premise": "the hidden truth in one or two sentences",
 "people": [{"who": "{{ asker }}", "part": "the one who asks", "truth": "..."}, {"who": "p2", "part": "...", "truth": "..."}],
 "items": [] or [{"id": "i1", "item": "<a name from the list>", "name": "<its name in the story>", "hidden": "home" or "pocket" (or "at"/"ambush"), "of": "p2"}],
 "offer": {"words": "...", "ask": "...", "journal": "...",{% if rumored %} "rumor": "...",{% endif %} "reward": {{ reward }}, "days": 3},
 "steps": [
  {"id": "s1", "do": "talk", "with": "p2", "goal": "...", "outcomes": [{"if": "...", "then": "s2", "journal": "..."}, {"if": "...", "then": "s3", "journal": "..."}]},
  {"id": "s2", "do": "choose", "with": "{{ asker }}", "goal": "...", "question": "...", "options": [{"say": "...", "then": "end:good", "journal": "...", "effect": "befriend {{ asker }} p2"}, {"say": "...", "then": "end:other", "journal": "..."}]},
  {"id": "s3", "do": "find", "item": "i1", "goal": "...", "then": "s4", "journal": "..."},
  {"id": "s4", "do": "give", "item": "i1", "to": "{{ asker }}", "goal": "...", "then": "end:good", "journal": "..."}],
 "endings": {"good": {"journal": "...", "rumor": "...", "pay": true, "after": "...", "memories": {"{{ asker }}": "...", "p2": "..."}},
             "bad": {"journal": "...", "rumor": "...", "pay": false, "after": "...", "memories": {}},
             "other": {"journal": "...", "rumor": "", "pay": true, "after": "", "memories": {}}}}
